00:08:57,840 --> 00:09:00,320
Anyway, so measures of risk aversion.

219
00:09:00,320 --> 00:09:03,240
So remember we talked about expected value maximization

220
00:09:03,240 --> 00:09:05,000
versus expected utility theory.

221
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:06,960
So we've got expected value maximization

222
00:09:06,960 --> 00:09:08,880
versus expected utility theory.

223
00:09:08,880 --> 00:09:12,440
And the difference between them comes down to taste for risk.

224
00:09:17,720 --> 00:09:20,880
And now let us think about this a bit more deeply.

225
00:09:20,880 --> 00:09:24,920
Suppose that you're choosing between either x dollars,

226
00:09:24,920 --> 00:09:30,880
probability one, or you have x over q dollars

227
00:09:30,880 --> 00:09:32,880
with p is equal to q.

228
00:09:32,880 --> 00:09:36,160
So basically, you could either have, say, $10 of certainty,

229
00:09:36,160 --> 00:09:39,760
or you could have $100 with a 10% chance.

230
00:09:39,760 --> 00:09:42,000
Something like that.

231
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:45,400
Now, just as a matter of language,

232
00:09:45,400 --> 00:09:47,600
if you're indifferent between these two things,

233
00:09:47,600 --> 00:09:49,720
if you're indifferent between these two things,

234
00:09:49,720 --> 00:09:52,720
then you are risk neutral.

235
00:09:52,720 --> 00:09:55,120
If you're indifferent between these, you are risk neutral.

236
00:09:55,120 --> 00:09:57,200
If you prefer the sure thing to the gamble

237
00:09:57,200 --> 00:10:00,000
of the same expected value, you are, by definition,

238
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:01,800
risk averse.

239
00:10:01,800 --> 00:10:03,840
If you prefer the gamble to the sure thing,

240
00:10:03,840 --> 00:10:07,240
then you are, by definition, risk preferring.

241
00:10:07,240 --> 00:10:13,440
Sometimes people will say things like, well, I went and,

242
00:10:13,440 --> 00:10:17,600
this is going to be a really bad example, or rather

243
00:10:17,600 --> 00:10:19,720
a good example of a bad thought.

244
00:10:19,720 --> 00:10:23,320
It's like, I wear a seat belt because I'm risk averse.

245
00:10:23,320 --> 00:10:27,600
It's like, well, maybe you wear a seat belt just

246
00:10:27,600 --> 00:10:30,400
because you want to have some extra safety at a low cost.

247
00:10:30,400 --> 00:10:34,120
It doesn't mean that you have to be risk averse to do that.

248
00:10:34,120 --> 00:10:36,720
Risk aversion would not just be that you take any measure

249
00:10:36,720 --> 00:10:40,160
to avoid risk, but rather that you

250
00:10:40,160 --> 00:10:43,440
are willing to go and give up some,

251
00:10:43,440 --> 00:10:47,800
to reduce the expected value in order to reduce the variance.

252
00:10:47,800 --> 00:10:50,240
You'd rather settle for a somewhat lower mean

253
00:10:50,240 --> 00:10:51,600
in order to reduce the variance.

254
00:10:51,600 --> 00:10:53,960
And that's what is risk averse.

255
00:10:53,960 --> 00:10:57,440
So it is not true that everyone is automatically risk averse.

256
00:10:57,440 --> 00:10:59,240
It's actually a very specific claim.

