00:11:14,200 --> 00:11:14,700
here?

263
00:11:14,700 --> 00:11:17,920
This is u, this is w, like that.

264
00:11:17,920 --> 00:11:19,320
All right.

265
00:11:19,320 --> 00:11:22,800
Now, if you're risk averse, what you will see

266
00:11:22,800 --> 00:11:26,960
is that you've got a concave utility wealth function.

267
00:11:26,960 --> 00:11:29,840
And if you've forgotten what that means, what it means

268
00:11:29,840 --> 00:11:33,520
is that the curve will always be above a straight line drawn

269
00:11:33,520 --> 00:11:35,720
between any of the two points.

270
00:11:35,720 --> 00:11:37,560
Curve is always above a straight line

271
00:11:37,560 --> 00:11:39,320
drawn between any two points.

272
00:11:39,320 --> 00:11:42,200
What this intuitively means, suppose this is outcome x,

273
00:11:42,200 --> 00:11:43,680
this is outcome y.

274
00:11:43,680 --> 00:11:46,320
What this is saying is that if you

275
00:11:46,320 --> 00:11:49,920
could get the average payoff with certainty,

276
00:11:49,920 --> 00:11:51,400
your utility is there.

277
00:11:51,400 --> 00:11:55,960
And you prefer that to having this probability of this

278
00:11:55,960 --> 00:11:57,320
and that probability of that one.

279
00:11:57,320 --> 00:12:00,280
So basically, if you were somewhere along that line,

280
00:12:00,280 --> 00:12:00,880
what is that?

281
00:12:00,880 --> 00:12:02,400
That's like a third of the way along.

282
00:12:02,400 --> 00:12:07,600
So you'd rather have 1 third x plus 2 thirds y with certainty

283
00:12:07,600 --> 00:12:11,040
than have a 1 third chance x of x and a 2 third chance of y.

284
00:12:11,040 --> 00:12:14,960
And that is the part of being risk averse.

285
00:12:14,960 --> 00:12:16,320
And on the other hand, of course,

286
00:12:16,320 --> 00:12:17,760
if your utility function like this

287
00:12:17,760 --> 00:12:21,880
would be the other way around, then you would be convex.

288
00:12:21,880 --> 00:12:25,200
And then the line would be above the utility function,

289
00:12:25,200 --> 00:12:27,280
indicating that you would rather have the gamble.

290
00:12:27,280 --> 00:12:29,400
You would rather have the gamble.

291
00:12:29,400 --> 00:12:32,280
And of course, if your utility function was a straight line,

292
00:12:32,280 --> 00:12:35,120
then if you draw a line between any two points

293
00:12:35,120 --> 00:12:38,160
in a straight line, you stay on the line,

294
00:12:38,160 --> 00:12:40,080
indicating that you are indifferent.

295
00:12:40,080 --> 00:12:42,760
Indicating that you're indifferent.

296
00:12:42,760 --> 00:12:46,600
Now, a related concept is the certainty equivalence.

297
00:12:46,600 --> 00:12:50,280
So if you're indifferent between a gamble and x star of certainty,

298
00:12:50,280 --> 00:12:52,720
then x star is, by definition, the certainty equivalent

299
00:12:52,720 --> 00:12:54,400
to that gamble.

300
00:12:54,400 --> 00:12:56,520
So think about any gamble and then say,

301
00:12:56,520 --> 00:12:58,080
what is the amount of money you could

302
00:12:58,080 --> 00:12:59,640
have with certainty that would make you indifferent

303
00:12:59,640 --> 00:13:01,600
between that amount and the gamble?

304
00:13:01,600 --> 00:13:05,120
And that is the certainty equivalence element.

305
00:13:05,120 --> 00:13:07,600
And if you think about the risk premium,

306
00:13:07,600 --> 00:13:11,320
the risk premium is just the difference

307
00:13:11,320 --> 00:13:15,360
between the expected value and the certainty equivalence.

308
00:13:18,600 --> 00:13:20,000
So basically, the expected value is

309
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:22,600
above the certainty equivalent, which it normally will be.

310
00:13:22,600 --> 00:13:24,160
Then there's a positive risk premium.

311
00:13:24,160 --> 00:13:27,560
You're willing to pay something to get rid of the risk.

312
00:13:27,560 --> 00:13:29,920
On the other hand, so if you really just love gambling,

313
00:13:29,920 --> 00:13:32,520
you couldn't bear to be, we couldn't

314
00:13:32,520 --> 00:13:34,440
bear a world of certainty.

315
00:13:34,440 --> 00:13:36,360
You were suffering in that world.

316
00:13:36,360 --> 00:13:38,560
Then you would actually have a negative risk premium.

317
00:13:38,560 --> 00:13:40,080
You say, hey, I'll pay something.

318
00:13:40,080 --> 00:13:41,520
I'll worsen the mean if I can just

319
00:13:41,520 --> 00:13:46,800
get some excitement in my life, just get some variance into it.

320
00:13:46,800 --> 00:13:48,760
All right, now there's a bunch of different ways

321
00:13:48,760 --> 00:13:50,920
you can quantify risk aversion.

322
00:13:50,920 --> 00:13:52,840
I think there's five different ways.

323
00:13:52,840 --> 00:13:55,840
There's only one that economists really ever use in practice.

324
00:13:55,840 --> 00:13:56,600
It looks like this.

325
00:13:56,600 --> 00:13:58,360
Use these frequently.

326
00:13:58,360 --> 00:14:00,120
So I'm just going to teach you that one.

327
00:14:00,120 --> 00:14:04,320
If you're curious, you can go and read about the other ones.

328
00:14:04,320 --> 00:14:07,960
I'd be surprised if anyone were, but great if you want to be.

329
00:14:07,960 --> 00:14:10,240
And this is called the coefficient

330
00:14:10,240 --> 00:14:12,480
of absolute risk aversion.

331
00:14:12,480 --> 00:14:15,320
That's the coefficient of absolute risk aversion.

332
00:14:25,120 --> 00:14:28,920
That's the coefficient of absolute risk aversion.

333
00:14:28,920 --> 00:14:34,080
And it is equal to the negative of the second derivative

334
00:14:34,080 --> 00:14:36,920
of the expected value function or expected utility function

335
00:14:36,920 --> 00:14:38,120
over the first derivative.

336
00:14:41,800 --> 00:14:43,520
And then the higher this coefficient is,

337
00:14:43,520 --> 00:14:44,800
the more risk averse you are.

338
00:14:44,800 --> 00:14:48,200
The higher, the more, a bigger positive number

339
00:14:48,200 --> 00:14:49,920
means more risk aversion.

340
00:14:49,920 --> 00:14:51,760
Zero means risk neutrality.

341
00:14:51,760 --> 00:14:54,720
And a negative number means that you're actually a risk averting.

342
00:14:54,720 --> 00:14:59,600
So if we had that u is equal to the square root of wealth,

343
00:14:59,600 --> 00:15:01,960
then the first derivative of that

344
00:15:01,960 --> 00:15:05,680
is going to be equal to 1 half w raised to the minus 1 half.

345
00:15:05,680 --> 00:15:10,320
Remember, when you're over, you can go to the opposite.

346
00:15:10,320 --> 00:15:14,000
The double prime could be equal to minus 1 quarter

347
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:18,920
w raised to the minus 3 halves.

348
00:15:18,920 --> 00:15:21,400
And then to get the coefficient of absolute risk aversion,

349
00:15:21,400 --> 00:15:23,320
we just take minus that over that.

350
00:15:23,320 --> 00:15:28,360
And then we find out it's going to be equal to 0.5 w raised

351
00:15:28,360 --> 00:15:29,680
to the negative 1.

352
00:15:29,680 --> 00:15:32,760
In other words, 0.5 over w.

353
00:15:32,760 --> 00:15:36,160
And is that thing going to be positive for all positive values

354
00:15:36,160 --> 00:15:37,840
of wealth?

355
00:15:37,840 --> 00:15:40,300
So you're always going to have a positive value of wealth.

356
00:15:40,300 --> 00:15:41,760
Otherwise, it's undefined, right?

357
00:15:41,760 --> 00:15:43,640
So utility is equal to the square root of w.

358
00:15:43,640 --> 00:15:45,440
It's not defined for a negative 1.

359
00:15:45,440 --> 00:15:46,120
Yes?

360
00:15:46,120 --> 00:15:48,920
And you said earlier, bigger positive number

361
00:15:48,920 --> 00:15:50,360
means greater risk aversion.

362
00:15:50,360 --> 00:15:51,200
Exactly.

363
00:15:51,200 --> 00:15:52,080
That's correct.

364
00:15:52,080 --> 00:15:53,240
So yes, the more positive.

365
00:15:53,240 --> 00:15:56,920
So a million means you have a coefficient of absolute risk

366
00:15:56,920 --> 00:15:57,400
aversion.

367
00:15:57,400 --> 00:15:58,240
A million is bigger.

368
00:15:58,240 --> 00:16:01,600
That means more risk aversion than 1,000.

369
00:16:01,600 --> 00:16:07,360
So what you see here is that with this utility function,

370
00:16:07,360 --> 00:16:10,400
for all levels of wealth, you are risk averse.

371
00:16:10,400 --> 00:16:12,560
For all levels of wealth, you are risk averse.

372
00:16:12,560 --> 00:16:14,080
Although you can also see that as w

373
00:16:14,080 --> 00:16:16,560
goes up, what happens to your coefficient of absolute risk

374
00:16:16,560 --> 00:16:18,400
aversion?

375
00:16:18,400 --> 00:16:18,960
Goes down.

376
00:16:18,960 --> 00:16:20,840
You become less and less risk averse.

377
00:16:20,840 --> 00:16:23,880
And as w goes to infinity, then your risk aversion goes to what?

378
00:16:23,880 --> 00:16:24,380
0.

379
00:16:24,380 --> 00:16:24,880
0.

380
00:16:24,880 --> 00:16:27,400
Risk aversion goes to 0.

381
00:16:27,400 --> 00:16:31,720
Now, if we just had utility equals w,

382
00:16:31,720 --> 00:16:35,720
then we would see that the first derivative would be equal to 1

383
00:16:35,720 --> 00:16:38,160
and the second derivative equal to 0.

384
00:16:38,160 --> 00:16:40,760
And then our coefficient of absolute risk aversion

385
00:16:40,760 --> 00:16:42,440
would be negative.

386
00:16:42,440 --> 00:16:46,560
That equals negative 0 over 1 equals 0.

387
00:16:46,560 --> 00:16:50,720
So what we see here is that with any linear utility function,

388
00:16:50,720 --> 00:16:54,160
you're going to have a coefficient of absolute risk

389
00:16:54,160 --> 00:16:57,120
aversion, which of 0.

390
00:16:57,120 --> 00:16:59,600
So any time that your utility is just

391
00:16:59,600 --> 00:17:01,280
a linear function of your wealth,

392
00:17:01,280 --> 00:17:04,440
then you are automatically risk neutral.

393
00:17:04,440 --> 00:17:09,120
And then as you might guess, is so remember that linear function

394
00:17:09,120 --> 00:17:11,560
means that the coefficient or that it's

395
00:17:11,560 --> 00:17:13,760
w raised to the power of 1.

396
00:17:13,760 --> 00:17:16,840
And what you might guess, which you can confirm for yourself,

397
00:17:16,840 --> 00:17:19,080
is that if the coefficient there is less than 1,

398
00:17:19,080 --> 00:17:21,080
then you're going to be risk averse.

399
00:17:21,080 --> 00:17:23,380
On the other hand, if the coefficient's greater than 1,

400
00:17:23,380 --> 00:17:25,160
then you will be risk averse.

401
00:17:25,160 --> 00:17:27,840
So if you were to do w squared, that

402
00:17:27,840 --> 00:17:29,640
would be a risk-preferring utility function.

403
00:17:29,640 --> 00:17:32,680
But so would u is equal to w raised to the 1.1.

404
00:17:32,680 --> 00:17:35,560
That would also be a risk-preferring utility

405
00:17:35,560 --> 00:17:37,680
function.

406
00:17:37,680 --> 00:17:39,960
And this here captures the intuition

407
00:17:39,960 --> 00:17:43,080
that normally as wealth goes up, risk aversion goes down.

408
00:17:43,080 --> 00:17:44,840
And the idea that a billionaire is

409
00:17:44,840 --> 00:17:47,120
more willing to gamble a dollar than someone

410
00:17:47,120 --> 00:17:50,040
on the edge of starvation, which makes some sense.

411
00:17:50,040 --> 00:17:53,040
So everyone happy with this?

412
00:17:53,040 --> 00:17:55,040
This is all we ever have to do with this.

413
00:17:55,040 --> 00:17:57,040
And I think this is all you'll ever have to know.

414
00:17:57,040 --> 00:17:59,040
So as long as you just know coefficient

415
00:17:59,040 --> 00:18:02,600
of absolute risk aversion, of 0 means that you are risk neutral.

416
00:18:02,600 --> 00:18:04,400
Positive means that you're risk averse.

417
00:18:04,400 --> 00:18:06,360
And negative means that you're risk preferring.

418
00:18:06,360 --> 00:18:08,600
Then you've got the basics down.

419
00:18:08,600 --> 00:18:10,320
And again, if you remember that formula,

420
00:18:10,320 --> 00:18:12,640
then you could even go and calculate

421
00:18:12,640 --> 00:18:14,640
what the coefficient of absolute risk aversion

422
00:18:14,640 --> 00:18:16,640
is for any given utility function.

423
00:18:16,640 --> 00:18:18,640
And hopefully, you'll be able to do that.

424
00:18:18,640 --> 00:18:20,640
And you'll be able to calculate the utility function

425
00:18:20,640 --> 00:18:21,640
at the level of wealth.

426
00:18:21,640 --> 00:18:23,640
And you can remember that.

427
00:18:23,640 --> 00:18:25,640
All right, so questions about that?

428
00:18:25,640 --> 00:18:26,640
Happy?

429
00:18:26,640 --> 00:18:28,640
Thank you.

430
00:18:28,640 --> 00:18:29,640
All right.

431
00:18:29,640 --> 00:18:32,640
Now, one application of expected utility theory

432
00:18:32,640 --> 00:18:35,640
is solving textbook problems about insurance.

